# Target C: Woodall vertex covers force contact-stress concentration

**Date:** 2026-10-02\
**Base main:** \`abdbe1f5cd53806ab63f581df61e561273a03141\`\
**Status:** proof-only exact KKT consequence. C-II and Level I remain open.

## 1. Setup

Work with the canonical post-ZF C-II source and a relative-interior normalized
full-support KKT stress. Let

\[
H=(B,E_H)
\]

be the full actual diameter graph and let

\[
\nu=\nu(H),\qquad \tau=\tau(H)
\]

be its matching and vertex-cover numbers.

Write the normalized diameter multipliers as

\[
\beta_e>0,
\qquad
\sum_{e\in E_H}\beta_e=1.
\tag{1}
\]

For a loaded terminal \(v\), define its incident diameter mass

\[
s_v:=\sum_{e\ni v}\beta_e
\tag{2}
\]

and its diameter reaction vector

\[
g_v
:=
\sum_{vw\in E_H}
\beta_{vw}(p_v-p_w).
\tag{3}
\]

KKT equilibrium gives

\[
g_v
=
\sum_{vy\in C}
\alpha_{vy}(p_v-p_y),
\qquad
\alpha_{vy}>0.
\tag{4}
\]

Canonical C26 gives

\[
\deg_C(v)\le3
\tag{5}
\]

for every loaded terminal.

Current main proves the Woodall cover estimate

\[
\boxed{\tau\le\nu+1.}
\tag{6}
\]

The new observation is to apply this cover to **KKT mass**, not only to diameter-edge
incidence.

## 2. A small diameter cover intercepts unit total KKT mass

Let \(S\subseteq B\) be a minimum vertex cover of \(H\), so \(|S|=\tau\).

Every diameter edge has at least one endpoint in \(S\). Therefore

\[
\sum_{v\in S}s_v
=
\sum_{e\in E_H}
\beta_e\,|\{ \text{endpoints of }e\text{ lying in }S\}|
\ge
\sum_{e\in E_H}\beta_e
=1.
\tag{7}
\]

Hence some \(v\in S\) satisfies

\[
\boxed{
s_v\ge{1\over\tau}\ge{1\over\nu+1}.
}
\tag{8}
\]

This step is exact and uses no lower bound on any individual diameter multiplier.

## 3. Diameter reactions at one endpoint cannot cancel

Fix a loaded terminal \(v\). All of its diameter partners lie on the radius-\(D\)
circle centered at \(v\).

If \(vw_1,vw_2\) are two diameter edges at \(v\), the smaller angle \(\theta\) between
their rays obeys

\[
\theta\le{\pi\over3}.
\tag{9}
\]

Indeed the opposite endpoints satisfy \(|w_1-w_2|\le D\), while

\[
|w_1-w_2|
=
2D\sin(\theta/2).
\]

Thus all diameter vectors

\[
p_v-p_w
\]

lie in one cone of aperture at most \(\pi/3\).

Let \(n_v\) be the unit bisector of a smallest such cone. Every incident diameter
vector then has projection at least

\[
D\cos{\pi\over6}
=
{\sqrt3\over2}D
\]

onto \(n_v\). Therefore

\[
g_v\cdot n_v
\ge
{\sqrt3\over2}D\,s_v,
\]

and hence

\[
\boxed{
|g_v|
\ge
{\sqrt3\over2}D\,s_v.
}
\tag{10}
\]

This is a finite estimate; the stronger \(1-o(1)\) alignment from the current
midpoint/star-aperture theorems is not needed.

## 4. Contact multiplier floor forced by matching number

Apply (10) to the vertex \(v\) from (8). Then

\[
|g_v|
\ge
{\sqrt3\over2}\,{D\over\nu+1}.
\tag{11}
\]

Equation (4), unit contact lengths, and the triangle inequality give

\[
\sum_{vy\in C}\alpha_{vy}
\ge
|g_v|.
\tag{12}
\]

Since \(\deg_C(v)\le3\), at least one actual unit contact \(e=vy\) satisfies

\[
\alpha_e
\ge
{|g_v|\over3}.
\]

Thus:

### Theorem 4.1 -- weighted-cover stress concentration

Every canonical post-ZF C-II source admits, in every relative-interior normalized
full-support KKT stress, an actual unit contact with

\[
\boxed{
\alpha_{\max}
\ge
{\sqrt3\over6}\,{D\over\nu(H)+1}.
}
\tag{13}
\]

Equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
\nu(H)+1
\ge
{\sqrt3\over6}\,{D\over\alpha_{\max}}.
}
\tag{14}
\]

No contact-stress upper bound is asserted.

## 5. Consequences for the hard diameter-matching regimes

If

\[
\nu(H)=o(D),
\]

then

\[
\boxed{\alpha_{\max}\to\infty.}
\tag{15}
\]

If, more sharply,

\[
\nu(H)\le K\sqrt D
\]

for a fixed \(K\), then

\[
\boxed{
\alpha_{\max}
\ge
\left({\sqrt3\over6K}-o(1)\right)\sqrt D.
}
\tag{16}
\]

Thus the square-root diameter branch cannot distribute normalized KKT stress
microscopically over all unit contacts. It necessarily contains a loaded terminal
incident with a superconstant, in fact square-root-scale, contact multiplier.

This removes the mass split in the earlier leaf/core theorem. That theorem obtained
an \(\Omega(\sqrt D)\) contact multiplier only when diameter mass avoided the pendant
leaf edges and concentrated on the core. The weighted-cover argument proves the same
scale **regardless of where the diameter multiplier mass lies**.

## 6. Sharpening with the modern star-aperture theorem

Current main also gives a source-wide aperture

\[
\varepsilon_D=o(1)
\]

for all diameter rays at one endpoint; the October cubic/midpoint estimates give an
explicit \(O(D^{-1/3})\) choice.

Repeating Section 3 with this aperture yields

\[
|g_v|
\ge
D s_v\cos(\varepsilon_D/2)
=
(1-o(1))Ds_v.
\]

Hence the asymptotically sharper form is

\[
\boxed{
\alpha_{\max}
\ge
\left({1\over3}-o(1)\right){D\over\nu+1}.
}
\tag{17}
\]

At \(\nu\le K\sqrt D\),

\[
\boxed{
\alpha_{\max}
\ge
\left({1\over3K}-o(1)\right)\sqrt D.
}
\tag{18}
\]

The finite constant (13) is independent of the October aperture theorem; (17) is a
stronger asymptotic corollary.

## 7. Contact-type fork

The contact \(e=vy\) in Theorem 4.1 starts at the loaded terminal \(v\). Therefore
exactly one of the following holds.

1. \(y\) is loaded, so \(e\) is a loaded-loaded unit contact.
2. \(y\) is unloaded, so \(e\) is a loaded-unloaded attachment contact.

In the canonical full-core near-disk branch, every loaded-loaded unit contact is a
true loaded hull side. Therefore the square-root hard branch forces either

\[
\boxed{\text{a hull-side multiplier of order }\sqrt D}
\]

or

\[
\boxed{\text{a loaded-unloaded attachment multiplier of order }\sqrt D.}
\]

This is a weighted analogue of the support collar fork, but it needs only one contact.

## 8. Exact virial normalization

For reference, summing the equilibrium equations after dotting with the vertex
positions gives the standard stress identity

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{e\in C}\alpha_e
=
D^2\sum_{h\in E_H}\beta_h
=
D^2.
}
\tag{19}
\]

Indeed every unit contact contributes \(\alpha_e|p_i-p_j|^2=\alpha_e\), and every
diameter edge contributes \(\beta_hD^2\).

Thus (16) is compatible with the global normalization: an \(\Omega(\sqrt D)\) contact
multiplier is large relative to a typical boundary-scale multiplier but still far
below the total \(D^2\) contact stress.

A closing argument must use where this large multiplier can propagate, not merely its
existence.

## 9. Next geometric target

At a loaded hull vertex in the near-disk full-core branch, the two hull-side unit
directions are almost tangent while the diameter reaction is almost radial.

Therefore a large hull-side multiplier cannot terminate locally unless either

- a comparable multiplier propagates into the next hull-side contact, or
- the third allowed loaded contact is an interior attachment carrying a comparable
  balancing force.

The theorem above supplies the initial \(\Omega(\sqrt D)\) seed. A useful next
result is a quantitative propagation lemma turning that seed into either

1. a long high-stress loaded-hull chain, or
2. many / large loaded-unloaded attachment stresses charged to one harmonic cell.

That propagation is not proved here.

**Disposition:** proof-only exact KKT reduction. No computation, numerical search,
verifier execution, or CI output is used.

